A||N||6||Heisenhund||Dumb Hose Question||||5||
Z||000000||Heisenhund||06-23-2000||07:23 PM||||Hello all,<P>Changed the water pump in my little money pit today, in an effort to try to get a handle on this cooling problem. While removing the hose from the pump, I noticed it had "OUTLET TO ENGINE" written on it. From the factory, not in chalk or white-out or blood or anything. However, it seem to go to the coolant pipe on the passenger side of the car. My dumb question is....<P>Is this right??<P>Heis<P>P.S.<BR>If anyone can send me an idea of the proper hose routing for this little beast, I'd be much appreciative.<BR>:-)<BR>||||reg||5
Z||000001||StuGood||06-23-2000||11:12 PM||||Sorry about the slow response.<P>Dumb question:  "Is this right?"<BR>Dumb answer: Yes, passenger side of car is RIGHT; driver's side is LEFT  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/wink.gif">.<P>Next dumb question: Please specify 4-cyl money pit or V6 money pit?<P>My memory tells me the water pump hose on the V6 goes to coolant pipe on RHS (passenger side) of vehicle.  If you've got a 4-cyl, probably the same, but I'd have to go look @ my 1988 SC.  Probably tomorrow morning.   <BR>||||reg||5
Z||000002||My7Fieros||06-23-2000||11:24 PM||||ummm, I dont have a hose comming out of my water pump on my 88 Formula ( I just changed it)..the only place I even see a place for one is on the "11:00" position if you're staring right at it from the side. It was pluged off though, as it is now.....what is it for?||||reg||5
Z||000003||Heisenhund||06-24-2000||12:06 AM||||The little money pit is of the 6 cylinder variety, currently packaged in an 86 GT. The big money pit is the 3 bedroom variety, currently attempting to crumble about my ears in Charleston, South Carolina.<P>The 11 o'clock position is where this hose sprouts out of the water pump, heads aft to connect up with a coolant pipe on the passenger (yes RIGHT) side. The reason I'm asking is that from outward appearance it would seem that would be a SUCTION for the pump, seeing as it comes FROM the radiator.<P>Or is my presumed flowpath out to lunch?<P>Heis<BR>||||reg||1
Z||000004||StuGood||06-24-2000||01:20 PM||||Good morning (almost)-!<BR>I just looked at my 1986 SE (V6), which has never had water pump nor hoses changed since I bought it new.  Everything's just as it came from the factory.<P>The small hose sprouting out of the pump at 11 o-clock position has an arrow printed on the hose pointing inward toward the pump, along with the words "INLET TO ENGINE."<BR>That's right - INLET.<P>This same hose runs aft, does a little S-curve under the dog bone, then heads outboard and disappears into the sidewall of engine compartment just aft of the strut tower.<P>Hope this helps  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/smile.gif">. <BR>||||reg||7
Z||000005||Heisenhund||06-24-2000||01:26 PM||||AHA!!!!<P>I think I have identified the culprit. I believe it to be a heater pipe (not a coolant pipe) that this little devil goes to. <P>Mine is the same as yours Stu (imagine that, 2 86 V-6's with the same setup!!).<P>If anyone could verify that my newest assumption is correct, I can kill this thread and log it as another Fiero experience.<P>Heis<BR>||||reg||10
Z||000006||StuGood||06-24-2000||02:07 PM||||I'm sure you're right.<P>Good thing, too.  You'll need the heater this winter, especially if the big money pit crumbles to the extent that you are forced to live in your little money pit   <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/biggrin.gif"> ! <P>My7Fieros: My 1987 GT has the plug in the water pump's 11-o'clock port, too.  It would appear that, for 1987-88 cars, return  from the heater core "tees" into the main coolant pipe on passenger side. That way, they saved the cost of another chunk of stainless tubing for the heater return.<P>And why not?  It seems to work just fine.  Some engineer saved the company $, and was probably rewarded with a paper certificate in a handsome, simulated-wood plastic frame  <IMG SRC="http://www.fiero.nl/forum/rolleyes.gif"> !     <BR><p>[This message has been edited by StuGood (edited 06-24-2000).]||||reg||14
